DCTelnet 2.0-UP/X (unofficial release, 1.11.2026)
Based on upstream DCTelnet 2.0-dev by Bruno Frederic.

New Features:
- Built-in renderer (Display > Renderer), default on 68020 and better:
  ANSI, VT and xterm sequences, UTF-8, 256-colour and RGB text, box and
  block characters, mouse reports (with the NewMouse wheel), bracketed
  paste, window titles, scrollback with Shift+Page Up/Down and the mouse
  wheel. It works on RTG and 256-colour AGA screens and keeps its text when
  the display reopens.
- SSH runs on ssh.library; the Installer copies the build for your CPU
  (68000, 68020, 68040 or 68060) to LIBS:. The host gets the terminal name
  xterm or ansi instead of VT102.
- Terminal Type (Automatic, BBS, Unix, PETSCII) and iCE Colours, with a PC
  ANSI personality
- ANSI music follows the GW-BASIC PLAY rules of SyncTERM; it is on in a
  first run's preferences, and text after a foreground tune waits until
  the tune has played
- PETSCII keys: capitals, Ctrl and Commodore colour keys
- PETSCII Mode for Commodore 64 BBSes, with the Petscii and PetsciiLower
  fonts (Terminal menu); a character set switch changes the whole screen,
  as on a C64
- Modern Icons tool bar (Display menu)
- Menu check marks and local echo follow the session
- Per-BBS settings: each Address Book entry can override four groups of
  settings (Screen, Terminal, Keyboard, Transfer), and has a comment and
  a login macro. Connection > Save Settings to Address Book Entry saves what
  was changed during a connection.
- 256-colour AGA and RTG (Picasso96/CyberGraphX) screen modes; the 16 ANSI
  colours get their own pens, also on the Workbench
- Palette editor for the 16 ANSI colours, with live preview (also on
  true-colour screens)
- DCTelnet's own screen has a grey interface and a blue title bar; a font
  or palette change there reopens only the terminal
- TopazPro font for square-pixel screen modes
- 80-column terminal: the Workbench window opens 80x25 and does not grow
  wider than 80 columns; on a wider screen the terminal is centred
- The BBS is told the real text area (NAWS), and again after every resize
  or font change
- Screen Font changes in place (ibmcon.device and console.device renderers),
  without closing the screen or the window
- Select text with the mouse; Edit > Copy (Right-Amiga+C), Paste
  (Right-Amiga+V) and Copy Screen; Reset Screen is now Right-Amiga+Z
- Capture to File and Save Screen (DCTelnet menu); find text in the Scroll
  Back (F2)
- Redial, keep alive and connect timeout (Settings > Connection Options);
  the login macro's waits no longer freeze the window
- Rlogin connections (Terminal > Rlogin), with automatic login on
  Synchronet
- SSH connections (Terminal > SSH), with known server keys in
  PROGDIR:KnownHosts; DCTelnet says so when an SSH connect reaches a
  Telnet server
- Character Set: UTF-8 BBSes and hosts (also when a character is split
  between two network reads), Amiga BBS text copied as it is
- Bell as a flash, a sound or off, also in PETSCII sessions
- 132 Columns
- Import a SyncTERM phone book into the Address Book
- An ARexx port (DCTELNET.1) with CONNECT, SEND, WAITFOR and more; the
  login macro can wait for a text (\w"Password:"). ARexx keeps being
  served while a connect is in progress.
- Programs that ask where the cursor is (Device Status Report) get an
  answer, so BBS animations and countdowns no longer stall
- ibmcon.device 1.11: screen buffer, blinking text and reading text back
  from the screen; the console.device renderer gains colours, background
  and the C64 character sets
- Home, End, Page Up, Page Down and Insert keys (VT Keys option), and the
  cursor keys follow the host's cursor key mode
- Settings from 1.x versions load with all their settings and get colours
  for the built-in renderer; a 68000 or 68010 starts with the
  ibmcon.device renderer
- DCTelnet uses the newest ibmcon.device and libraries from its own drawer
  or from DEVS: / LIBS:; the package has Devs, Fonts and Libs drawers
- The start-up screen shows the display engine, screen and text area
- The cursor is a solid block; shell prompt arrows show as '>'

Bug Fixes:
- Menu items were grey, as if disabled, on screens with 32+ colours
- Menus were hard to read on AmigaOS 2.x
- Deleting the first Address Book entry left no selection
- Typing in a field entered with Tab now replaces its text
- Finger replies are no longer shown as a staircase
- F1 in PETSCII Mode sent the code of F2
- Shift+F1-F10 sent the macro of the next key and a "~"; Shift+Cursor
  keys and Help sent stray characters
- Text requesters (Connect, Find ...) needed Return twice
- Codes such as CSI ?25h left "25h" in the Scroll Back
- SSH sessions echoed twice and Backspace did not work
- Answers from XEM libraries did not reach the BBS
- ibmcon.device: Insert Lines did not scroll the whole region, and closing
  the device freed DCTelnet's signal bit 31
- An 8-bit control character in pasted text could end a bracketed paste
  early
- The Terminal Type label overlapped the Characters gadget
- The tool bar icons were drawn for fixed pens and showed pink, cyan and
  blue on most screens. New flat, antialiased icons carry their own
  colours and are matched to every screen, in a square and a wide set.
